{
  "requirement": "Each language supplies the scheme identifying its structural-hash rules and\ntheir version. A change to those rules uses a different scheme; an update\npreserving the calculation can retain the scheme. Loaded contents retain the\nscheme used to compute their hashes.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait OutlineLang: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    /// Identify the structural-hash rules and their version.\n    fn fingerprint_scheme(&self) -> &str;\n\n    // ...\n}\n\nimpl OutlineContent {\n    /// The selected item's structural digest from this snapshot.\n    pub fn structural_hash(&self) -> &str;\n\n    /// The retained scheme identifying its hashing rules and version.\n    pub fn fingerprint_scheme(&self) -> &str;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The one marked test, `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[explicit_languages_share_configuration_and_retain_their_scheme]`, adequately checks `requirements.sdk.outlines.language_provider.fingerprint_scheme`. I did not read the implementation beyond search snippets.\n\nObligations and how the test covers them:\n- **Each language supplies a scheme.** The test's `CustomLanguage` returns \"custom-v2\" from `fingerprint_scheme`. Both construction paths consume it: `from_source_with` for the supplied contents and `from_path_with` with a cloned language for the loaded contents. Every root's `fingerprint().scheme` is asserted to be \"custom-v2:own-v1\", so the language's scheme reaches the contents. The bundled languages' scheme values are pinned in `bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline`. That test is marked for the per-language `requirements.sdk.sources.*.fingerprints` requirements, which cover those values.\n- **A change to the rules uses a different scheme.** A second language configured with \"custom-v3\" produces the same `structural_hash` for the selected item. Its `fingerprint()` is asserted `assert_ne!` to the \"custom-v2\" one. This shows the scheme is carried and distinguishes rule versions.\n- **An update that preserves the calculation can retain the scheme.** This is a MAY, so nothing is required.\n- **Loaded contents retain the scheme used to compute their hashes.**\n  - The language, its clone and the configuration `Arc` are all dropped. Then `supplied.fingerprint_scheme()` is asserted equal to \"custom-v2\", which shows the scheme is retained independently of the provider.\n  - The loaded and selected contents are checked through their fingerprints, whose scheme is built from the retained scheme. `loaded.select(..).fingerprint()` is asserted equal to `selected.fingerprint()`.\n  - The selected contents' fingerprint must differ from the \"custom-v3\" one even though their structural hashes match. A selection that lost or replaced the scheme would make those two fingerprints equal, so this assertion would fail.\n\nThe test does not call `fingerprint_scheme()` directly on the loaded or selected contents. It checks the scheme through `fingerprint().scheme` and `assert_ne!` instead. Those assertions would still reject a wrong or lost scheme, so I count this as covered rather than as a gap.\n\nNo {fixme} acknowledgments apply.",
